; Outer read isolation for the Codex process itself.
;
; Codex's own `--sandbox read-only` is a *write* boundary: SandboxPolicy::
; has_full_disk_read_access returns true for every variant, seatbelt.rs turns
; that into a blanket `(allow file-read*)`, and the profile it builds wraps the
; commands the agent runs rather than the agent. Nothing there stops the Codex
; process from reading the repository, a .env file or an SSH key.
;
; This profile is applied to the Codex process itself, before it starts. The
; harness composes it after the platform section rather than using
; `(import ...)`: sandbox-exec resolves an import against its own search path,
; not against the directory the profile was loaded from.
;
; Paths arrive as `-D` parameters and must be *resolved* real paths: seatbelt
; matches the real filesystem, so /tmp would never match /private/tmp.

; ---- harness read allowlist ------------------------------------------------

; The Codex binary and the resources it ships with.
(allow file-read* file-map-executable (subpath (param "CODEX_VENDOR")))

; The empty evaluation workspace.
(allow file-read* (subpath (param "WORKSPACE")))

; The isolated CODEX_HOME. Holds the login state and nothing else.
(allow file-read* (subpath (param "CODEX_HOME")))

; The output schema, inherited as a descriptor. `load_output_schema` reads it
; once, so a single-read stream is the right transport for it.
; Nothing under the scratch directory is readable by path.
(allow file-read* (subpath "/dev/fd"))

; The pinned model catalog, as a named file rather than a descriptor.
;
; Codex 0.153.4 reads `model_catalog_json` twice: once in the initial
; `ConfigBuilder::build()` and again at `thread/start`, through
; `ConfigManager::load_with_overrides`. Reopening `/dev/fd/N` lands on the same
; open file description, so the second read of the descriptor the first read
; consumed returns nothing -- which is exactly how the third real probe died
; ("EOF while parsing a value at line 1 column 0"). A named file can be opened
; from scratch as often as the CLI asks.
;
; `literal`, never `subpath`: the directory holding it must stay closed, so
; nothing else becomes readable by being placed beside the catalog. Read only,
; and deliberately no `file-write-*` of any kind -- the runtime catalog is the
; judged, digest-bound copy, and Codex may not rewrite, truncate, chmod,
; rename or unlink it.
(allow file-read* (literal (param "CATALOG_FILE")))

; ---- harness write allowlist -----------------------------------------------

; Exactly two files, both named literally, and only their contents. The
; directory itself stays unwritable, so no third file can appear beside them,
; and the user's own CODEX_HOME is not in the profile at all. Verified offline:
; both isolated copies can be read and rewritten, while creating any other file
; in the same directory is refused.

; Codex 0.153.4 persists a refreshed ChatGPT token through
; FileAuthStorage::save, which truncates and rewrites CODEX_HOME/auth.json;
; without this the refresh would fail inside the sandbox.
(allow file-write-data file-write-flags file-write-times
  (literal (param "AUTH_FILE")))

; `codex exec` starts an in-process app-server client, and its
; start_uninitialized calls resolve_installation_id
; (core/src/installation_id.rs), which opens CODEX_HOME/installation_id
; read+write+create, locks it, repairs its mode to 0644 and may rewrite it.
; Without this the second authorised real probe died before any event with
; "failed to initialize in-process app-server client: Operation not permitted".
;
; file-write-mode is here because the CLI repairs the mode, and the harness
; seeds the file at 0644 only so that repair is a no-op rather than a
; dependency. The operations were measured rather than copied from the rule
; above: with data/flags/times alone the chmod is refused, and `file-write*`
; would additionally permit *unlinking* this file. What is granted is the
; smallest set that works.
(allow file-write-data file-write-flags file-write-times file-write-mode
  (literal (param "INSTALLATION_ID_FILE")))

; ---- network ---------------------------------------------------------------

; The attempt has to reach the provider, so outbound TCP is open. Seatbelt
; matches sockets, not hostnames: there is no dependable way to narrow this to
; one endpoint here, and pretending otherwise would be worse than saying so.
;
; This is a deliberate, documented limit. What the outer sandbox enforces is the
; *file read* boundary; the network is the one thing the turn cannot do without.
(allow network-outbound (remote tcp))
(allow system-socket)
(allow mach-lookup
  (global-name "com.apple.dnssd.service")
  (global-name "com.apple.SystemConfiguration.configd"))

; The resolver socket, and nothing else in that directory.
;
; `getaddrinfo` does not send the DNS query itself. It hands the name to
; mDNSResponder over a Unix domain socket, and `(remote tcp)` does not cover a
; Unix socket -- neither does `(remote udp)`. So every outbound TCP connection
; by address worked while every connection by name failed, which is how the
; sixth real probe died after `turn.started`:
;
;   Reconnecting... 2/5 (stream disconnected before completion: failed to
;   lookup address information: nodename nor servname provided, or not known)
;
; Measured rather than guessed. Codex's own `seatbelt_network_policy.sbpl`
; lists `SystemConfiguration.DNSConfiguration`, `networkd`, `ocspd`,
; `SecurityServer` and the `net.routetable` sysctls; each was granted on its
; own against a resolver probe under this profile and not one of them made a
; remote name resolvable. `(allow network-outbound)` did, and narrowing that
; down left exactly this socket.
;
; The path is the resolved one. `/var/run` is a symlink to `/private/var/run`,
; and the unresolved spelling was measured and does not match -- the same rule
; the rest of this profile follows.
(allow network-outbound (literal "/private/var/run/mDNSResponder"))

; ---- TLS trust anchors -----------------------------------------------------

; Reaching the provider is not the same as being able to verify it. The eighth
; real probe resolved the name, opened the connection, started a turn, and then
; spent its whole budget on:
;
;   Reconnecting... 4/5 (stream disconnected before completion:
;                        invalid peer certificate: UnknownIssuer)
;
; Codex 0.153.4 uses `rustls-native-certs` 0.8.3, which on macOS reads the
; native store through `security_framework::trust_settings::TrustSettings` for
; the User, Admin and System domains -- not a bundled PEM file. Under this
; profile every domain came back empty, so every certificate chain was
; unknown.
;
; Measured, one dimension at a time. Codex's own network policy names
; `SecurityServer`, `networkd`, `ocspd` and `trustd.agent` for TLS certificate
; information; with file reads held open, only `SecurityServer` made the system
; domain readable, and `networkd`, `ocspd`, `securityd`, `coreservicesd` and
; the distributed notification service each made no difference. With mach held
; open, only `/System/Library/Keychains` did, and `/Library/Keychains`,
; `/private/var/db`, `/private/var/db/mds` and `/private/etc` each made none.
; Neither dimension works alone: both of these are required together.
;
; Narrowed to two literals rather than the directory. `X509Anchors` sits beside
; them and was measured to be unnecessary, so it is not granted.
(allow mach-lookup (global-name "com.apple.SecurityServer"))
(allow file-read*
  (literal "/System/Library/Keychains/SystemRootCertificates.keychain")
  (literal "/System/Library/Keychains/SystemTrustSettings.plist"))

; What is deliberately *not* here: `~/Library/Keychains`, `/Library/Keychains`,
; any user keychain, `HOME`, and any subpath of a trust or security directory.
; The attempt reads the public root anchors this machine already trusts and
; nothing that belongs to the user. The User domain stays empty inside the
; sandbox -- the isolated `HOME` holds no keychain -- and that is the intended
; outcome rather than a gap.
